---
import PrimerChat from "../PrimerChat.astro";
import { aiCardId, archiveAi } from "../../../lib/archive-ai";

const card = archiveAi.decision;
const price = { monday: 40, tuesday: 35 };
const cutoff = 40;
const disputeDays = 90;
const inReport = (p: number) => p < cutoff;

const days = [
  { day: "Monday", price: price.monday },
  { day: "Tuesday", price: price.tuesday },
];
const policies = [
  { id: "latest", name: "Latest only", kept: ["Tuesday"] },
  { id: "every", name: "Every capture", kept: ["Monday", "Tuesday"] },
];
const questions = [
  { q: "What prices did we see in the latest fetch?", needs: "Tuesday" },
  { q: "Rebuild Tuesday's report", needs: "Tuesday" },
  { q: "Why was the kettle missing from Monday's report?", needs: "Monday" },
];

const checks = [
  ["Overwritten", "Tuesday’s fetch overwrites Monday’s list. Which morning’s report can you rebuild from the saved list, assuming you have the code and configuration for both days?"],
  ["A recorded hash", "You kept Monday’s hash and someone sends you a file they say is Monday’s list. What can the hash help you check, and what could it do if nobody had a copy of the file?"],
  ["A dispute window", `The shop can dispute reports for up to ${disputeDays} days. What would you retain for that period so you could reproduce a disputed report even after the cutoff rule changes?`],
];
---

<PrimerChat
  prompt={card.prompt}
  module="Module 2"
  back={`../02-archive/#${aiCardId("decision")}`}
  about="This is a reply an AI assistant gave to Module 2's archive-history study prompt, lightly edited. Send the same prompt yourself and you will get a different reply."
  turn="In a live chat, you would answer those three questions and the assistant would review your reasoning. To try that, copy the prompt into your own AI assistant. Then go back to Part 6 and pick a policy for the league office."
>
  <h2 class="pr-title">How much history should an archive keep?</h2>
  <p>Suppose your archive fetches an online shop’s price list every morning at 8 a.m. On Monday, a kettle costs ${price.monday}. On Tuesday, it costs ${price.tuesday}. Your archive uses each morning’s list to publish a report of items under ${cutoff}.</p>

  <h2>1. What each policy can rebuild</h2>
  <p>If you <strong>keep only the latest copy</strong>, Tuesday’s fetch overwrites Monday’s. You can use the saved list to rebuild Tuesday’s report. If someone later asks whether the kettle appeared in Monday’s report, Tuesday’s list can’t tell you.</p>
  <p>If you <strong>keep every copy with its fetch time</strong>, you can retrieve the Monday and Tuesday lists and use each to rebuild that morning’s report. The timestamps tell you when your archive observed each price. A price change between morning fetches could go entirely unrecorded.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card pa-cap">
    <fieldset class="pr-seg">
      <legend>Archive policy</legend>
      {policies.map((p, i) => <label><input type="radio" name="pa-cap-policy" value={p.id} checked={i === 0} /><span>{p.name}</span></label>)}
    </fieldset>
    {policies.map((p) => (
      <div class="pa-cap-view" data-policy={p.id}>
        <p class="pr-label">The archive after Tuesday's fetch</p>
        <ol class="pa-cap-days">
          {days.map((d) => {
            const kept = p.kept.includes(d.day);
            return (
              <li class={kept ? "is-kept" : "is-gone"}>
                <span>{d.day} 8 a.m.</span>
                <b>Kettle ${d.price}</b>
                <small>{kept ? (inReport(d.price) ? "in the archive · kettle in that report" : "in the archive · kettle not in that report") : "overwritten by Tuesday's fetch"}</small>
              </li>
            );
          })}
        </ol>
        <p class="pr-label">Can it answer?</p>
        <ul class="pa-cap-qs">
          {questions.map((q) => {
            const ok = p.kept.includes(q.needs);
            return <li><span>{q.q}</span><span class:list={["pr-chip", ok ? "is-ok" : "is-bad"]}>{ok ? "Yes" : "No"}</span></li>;
          })}
        </ul>
      </div>
    ))}
  </figure>

  <h2>2. What a hash can show</h2>
  <p>A hash is a short fingerprint computed from the bytes of a file. If you record Monday’s hash and someone later hands you a candidate copy of Monday’s list, you can hash it and compare. A match with a strong hash is strong evidence that the bytes match what you originally hashed. A mismatch means the bytes differ, even if the only change was formatting.</p>
  <p>If Monday’s list was overwritten, its hash can still help you check a copy found elsewhere. It can’t recreate the list.</p>
  <p class="pr-pull">The fingerprint is much shorter than the file and doesn’t contain the information needed to recover it.</p>

  <h2>3. Let the questions drive retention</h2>
  <p>If the archive only needs to answer “What prices did we see in the latest fetch?”, keeping the latest copy is enough. If it also needs to answer “Why was this kettle missing from Monday’s report?”, it needs the inputs from that day.</p>
  <p>Start with how far back those questions can reach. If disputes can be raised for {disputeDays} days, you might keep {disputeDays} days of snapshots and compare the storage cost with the cost of being unable to resolve a dispute. If many days have identical lists, you can store the identical bytes once and keep a record of every fetch time. That preserves the history while reducing storage.</p>

  <h2>4. The list is only one ingredient</h2>
  <p>Suppose Monday’s code used “under ${cutoff},” but the rule later changed to “${cutoff} or less.” Running today’s code on Monday’s saved list would include the ${price.monday} kettle, even though Monday’s report excluded it. The same problem can happen if the rule stayed in the code but the cutoff changed in a configuration file.</p>
  <p>For an exact rebuild, record which source snapshot, code version, and configuration each report used. Preserve any other inputs that affect the result, such as the report date or the dependency versions, if they can change the output.</p>
  <aside class="pr-course" aria-label="Course note">
    <p class="pr-label">Course note</p>
    <p>Touchline keeps the latest capture of each page, with its fetch time: the reply's first policy. The decision's two policies both assume the parser, build code and config of every past run are kept in version control, the reply's section 4, so the pages are the only input whose past can be lost.</p>
    <p>The reply's word “fingerprint” here means a content hash of a file. In this course a fingerprint is the hash of the parser code and its config; a content hash identifies a saved page.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>5. Check your understanding</h2>
  <ol class="pr-checks">{checks.map(([name, q]) => <li><b>{name}</b><span>{q}</span></li>)}</ol>
</PrimerChat>

<style>
  .pa-cap .pr-seg { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
  .pa-cap-view { display: none; }
  .pa-cap:has([value="latest"]:checked) [data-policy="latest"],
  .pa-cap:has([value="every"]:checked) [data-policy="every"] { display: block; }
  .pa-cap-days { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 0.45rem; margin: 0 0 0.8rem; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .pa-cap-days li { display: grid; gap: 0.1rem; padding: 0.5rem 0.7rem; border: 1px solid var(--pr-green); border-radius: 8px; background: var(--tl-bg); font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .pa-cap-days li span { color: var(--tl-muted); }
  .pa-cap-days li b { color: var(--sl-color-white); font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .pa-cap-days li small { color: var(--tl-muted); }
  .pa-cap-days li.is-gone { border: 1.5px dashed var(--pr-red); background: transparent; }
  .pa-cap-days li.is-gone b { color: var(--tl-muted); text-decoration: line-through; }
  .pa-cap-qs { display: grid; gap: 0.35rem; margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .pa-cap-qs li { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; gap: 0.6rem; padding: 0.35rem 0.6rem; border-radius: 6px; background: var(--tl-bg); }
</style>
